# Preference-Based Search & Location-Context Adaptive Recommendations

## 1. Executive Summary

This document explains the architecture, mathematical scoring model, classification engine, and context-scoping mechanics of the **Preference-Based Search & Adaptive Recommendation System**.

### The Core Problem Solved
Traditional behavior-based recommenders track what a user taps globally. If a user clicks 10 beaches while staying in Galle, a traditional system keeps recommending beaches even when the user travels to **Kandy** (in the mountains) or **Anuradhapura** (an ancient heritage city) where no beaches exist!

### The Solution: Location-Context Scoping
Our system introduces a **3-Signal Adaptive Scoring Model**. Historical region behavior is **context-scoped**:
- When the user is in **Galle** (coastal context), their historical beach interest yields a **+5 region bonus** for coastal spots.
- When the same user travels to **Kandy** (hill context) or **Polonnaruwa** (heritage context), the beach region bonus is automatically **inhibited (0 bonus)**. Results adaptively shift to hill tops, tea plantations, or ancient temples matching their location and evolving search intent.
- In multi-trait cities like **Colombo** (possessing beaches, temples, and city life), an adaptive **Diversity Interleaver** prevents mono-topic results when interest scores are closely matched.

